      examTips: ["Know all three MSC components by name and know exactly which one is regulated and which two aren't — this specific distinction is a favourite exam angle.", "Be precise that a chargeback is issuer-initiated, not acquirer- or scheme-initiated — this exact detail is commonly tested and easy to get backwards under pressure."],
      memoryTrick: "MSC isn't one fee wearing different names — it's three separate line items stacked on top of each other: interchange, scheme fee, and acquirer margin.",
      keyPoints: ["An acquiring bank onboards merchants, underwrites their risk, connects them to card schemes, and passes on settled funds.", "The Merchant Service Charge is a bundle of three components — interchange fee, scheme fee, and acquirer margin — only one of which is regulated in the UK/EU.", "A chargeback is initiated by the issuer following a cardholder dispute, and can be contested by the merchant with supporting evidence.", "Acquirers carry genuine financial risk, particularly if a merchant fails or generates a persistently high rate of chargebacks."],
